polynonymous@gmail.com wrote:
>
> What do you people think about ascensions which people just
> genocide most of the threatening monsters, rather than dealing
> with them like Real Men? I've always considered this to be
> pretty abusive and I have no idea why the devs would have
> implemented this. I've seen many ascensions in this group
> that blessed genocided Ls, ;s, etc. That's just cheating if
> you ask me.

The stated goal of the game is to deliver the Amulet
to your diety.  Making it deliberately harder runs
contrary to that goal.  Thus players "should" do
whatever it takes to make the game easier and "should"
avoid doing whatever it takes to make the game harder.
Those are two huge "should"s, aren't they?

To me the primary purpose of playing the game is to
have fun and that means doing stuff that makes
ascension work but within my current skill level.  The
more my skill, the higher my ascension ratio, the
tougher my clallenges.

In general genociding monsters simply makes the random
monster generation routine create tougher monsters.
But that's a general trend, not a rule without any
exceptions.  There are a few types of monsters that are
capable well out of depth compared to their numerical
toughness.  They are the spell casters, the ones that
have instakills like ;, and the ones that give you
thousands of turns worth of setbacks like mind flayers
or disenchanters.  An optimal strategy is to
understand which monsters are out of depeth and which
are not, yet another of Nethack's many judgement
calls.  Some judge that none are out of depth enough
to justify genocide, others make the list large enough
to include soldier ants.  Somewhere between as where
the game is the least difficult and hence is the
optimal approach to ascend the most characters.  Not
that ascending the most characters is the most fun,
but it is the started goal.

> Another thing I wanted to bring up is wishing. Will this ever
> be fixed? You can wish for almost anything you want without being
> denied.

That word "almost" matters.  That items that some
want the most are the exceptions.  Ways to get infinte
wishes are plugged for example.

On the other hand there are an assortment of item
types that never appear at random.  I've had a Valk
use a wish on a "blessed rustproof +2 athame" to be
able to engrave the E-word without losing weapons.

> Furthermore, many times you can successfully wish for
> other classes' Quest artifacts, which is just weak.

Then don't do that.  Very many folks who've already
ascended one class don't.  Very who've never ascended
don't.  "Can" does NOT equal "should".

> I've seen
> people on n.a.o with 3-4 ascensions that used 10+ wishes.

Notice what they used them for.  For example the more
artifacts you have the less likely an artifact wish
will work.  Wishes for mundane items becomes a
tactical decision.

> People seem to use wishing as a substitute for skill...

Try that all you like.  In the end wishes do NOT
substitute for skill.  Nethack has a vast number of
temptation traps, and that's one of them.  Using
wishes as a substitute for skill is even more
tempting than wasting skill slots on some nice
seeming weapon.  Sure enough the more skill the less
often wishes are needed.

But what's your personal goal in the game?  If it is
to have the highest possible ascension ratio, then
add good skill plus tactical and strategic use of
wishes together and the result will be a higher
ratio.  Some want that, others do not.



